[ ] Publish clearhead_core to crates.io =publish-core $crates.io has clearhead_core 0.2.0 from 2026-04-12. The API has changed since, so bump by semver (0.3.0), cargo publish --dry-run first, and check the wasm32 target dependencies and features package cleanly. Versions are independent per repository (decided 2026-10-05).$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 <publish-grammar #01a10f0d-790b-7001-b625-193df342be41
[ ] Name the implemented spec version in clearhead --version =version-names-spec $Decided 2026-10-05: each repository has its own semver, so the CLI says which specification release it implements, for example clearhead 0.3.0 (spec v0.2.0). Derive it at build time, never hand-typed, so it cannot drift. SOURCE (2026-10-05): clearhead_core's \[package.metadata.clearhead] specification, which ships with the published crate; Core's CI tests against that tag and validate-pinned requires the spec submodule to be exactly it, so --version reads the one declaration the tests already prove.$ !3 ^2026-10-05T19:31 #01a10f0d-791b-752b-ad33-03e365266965
[ ] Build CLI release binaries with dist =cli-dist $dist (formerly cargo-dist, v0.33) declares targets in Cargo.toml and generates a GitHub workflow that builds both binaries on a tag and attaches archives named as binstall expects. First fix clearhead_cli repository: it names ClearHeadToDo-Devs/clearhead_cli.git, which is a 404, and binstall looks for release assets there. DECIDED 2026-10-06 (human: simplicity is a virtue of its own): one target, x86_64 Linux, and one build per target, the full default (sparql on) with both binaries, clearhead and clearhead-lsp. binstall resolves one artifact per crate, version and target, so feature variants would each need their own crate, for 3.2 MB (16.8 vs 13.6 MB release). Minimal is a source build, documented in the README: cargo install clearhead_cli --no-default-features. If the LSP is ever wanted alone, split it by binary into its own package, not by feature. Releases follow user-visible change, not a calendar (decided 2026-10-05).$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 <publish-core #01a10f0d-7929-703a-88e3-7a17a557697d
[ ] Release the CLI so cargo binstall downloads it =cli-release $crates.io has clearhead_cli 0.2.1 from 2025-12-24, so cargo binstall clearhead_cli builds that old version from source today. Bump to 0.3.0, tag, let the dist workflow publish the binaries, then cargo publish. Proof: on a machine without a Rust toolchain, cargo binstall clearhead_cli downloads rather than compiles (its log names the strategy), and clearhead --version shows 0.3.0 and the spec release.$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 <cli-dist <version-names-spec #01a10f0d-793a-77ed-9421-68448777e645
[ ] Lint the minimal CLI profile in CI =lint-minimal $Found 2026-10-06: cargo build --release -p clearhead_cli --no-default-features warns dead_code, and CI lints only default features, so the minimal profile drifts unseen. Add a clippy step with --no-default-features (-D warnings), then fix what it finds.$ !4 ^2026-10-06T10:41 #01a1124e-2a1c-74fe-ae3e-14f6701598ca
